WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
Let’s talk about the Nile River

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.000
and why Egypt and Ethiopia have been fighting over it.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.400
The problem is this dam 
being built in Ethiopia.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:12.880
Once it’s finished it’ll be the most powerful hydroelectric dam in Africa. 

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.000
But Egypt says its supply of water is 
in danger of drying up.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.200
After years of talks they’re 
now saying they’re close

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.920
to signing a deal on 
how to share the water.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.640
But there are lots of details 
that need to be worked out. 

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.200
So why has it been so hard
to end the battle for the Nile?

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.800
Its official name is the Grand 
Ethiopian Renaissance Dam.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.240
And it’s huge.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:50.680
When they’re done building it

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.040
the reservoir will be bigger 
than Greater London 

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.600
and cover almost 1,700 sq km.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.920
It's pretty much the largest project 

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:01.200
the government has 
ever embarked upon.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.560
The original cost of it was 
around $5 billion. 

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.440
It will probably be significantly 
more than that.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.240
But before we get into the dispute about the dam itself

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.920
it helps to understand the Nile 
basin and what’s at stake.
 

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.960
Eleven African countries are in it and 280 million people live there.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.960
The Nile is also, fun fact

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.880
the only major river that 
runs south to north.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.200
One of the river’s tributaries,
 the Blue Nile

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.240
starts off in Lake Tana in Ethiopia
 

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.840
and converges the White Nile in Sudan’s capital Khartoum. 
 
  

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.280
From there the Nile flows 
downstream to Egypt.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.600
There are several dams along the way, including Egypt’s Aswan dam.
 

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.600
But Ethiopia is building its 
power plant upstream

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.320
right here on the border with Sudan.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.560
Now as the name Renaissance suggests 

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:49.920
this dam is supposed to revive Ethiopia’s economy.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.000
It’ll provide electricity to 
millions of Ethiopians.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:56.720
And that’s big because right now more than two-thirds of the population

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:00.840
aren’t connected to the grid. That’s about 75 million people.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.800
Over several years it will produce
 6,000 megawatts.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:09.200
Ethiopia's current generating capacity is only 4,000 megawatts.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.480
So this dam is also going to generate 

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.160
more power for Ethiopia than it needs

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.880
meaning it can export electricity.
&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.440
That’s all very good news for Ethiopia.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.840
But if you ask Egypt this dam 
will be catastrophic.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.400
The Nile has been at the centre of Egyptian life for millenia.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.920
An entire ancient civilisation 
was built around it

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.320
and relied on the river for 
transport and irrigation.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.280
And that’s still the case today.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.160
The country relies on the Nile 
for almost all of its water.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.840
It’s a rain-starved, largely
 desert country.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.480
So obviously any potential interruptions to the flow of the Nile

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.920
is justifiably of huge concern for Egypt.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:59.160
Egypt controls most of the
 flow of the River Nile  

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.080
into its country, at least for now.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.840
Egypt’s biggest worry is how quickly Ethiopia will fill the reservoir.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:11.320
And our colleagues at Al Jazeera Labs have studied a few scenarios.
 

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.360
Say Ethiopia agrees to fill the reservoir over the next 10 years.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.080
AJ Labs says that would cut
 Egypt’s water supply 

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:22.400
by 14% and destroy 18% of its farmland.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.920
If Ethiopia fills the dam more 
quickly, say over seven years

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.960
one-third of Egypt’s 
farmland would be lost.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.720
And a five-year window would 
destroy half of that land.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:36.000
According to their simulations this 
is how the Egyptian delta

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.560
would change over those 10 years.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.520
So Ethiopia’s best-case scenario

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.840
filling the dam as soon as possible

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.600
is Egypt’s worst nightmare.

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.240
It’s no wonder these negotiations
 have been tough.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.800
It was during the Arab Spring in 2011

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.320
while Egypt had a revolution 
on its hands

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.600
that Ethiopia started building the dam.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.680
Ever since then Egyptian leaders 
have tried to stop the project

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.520
and military conflict never seemed far.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:09.320
“Last year, Ethiopia accused Egypt of sending rebels to neighbouring Eritrea 

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.800
to sabotage the dam, prompting another Nile-sharing country

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.160
Sudan, to send its troops to its border.”
 

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:18.600
In 2018 the operating manager 
of the Ethiopian dam

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.920
Simegnew Bekele, was found dead.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.680
“Bekele’s sudden death drew crowds and raised suspicions.”

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.680
It was reported he'd been shot in broad daylight which led to protests.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.520
But in the end the authorities said Bekele took his own life.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.120
In 2019 Ethiopia’s prime minister, 
Abiy Ahmed, even said:

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.960
"...no force could stop Ethiopia from building a dam.

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.600
If there is a need to go to war we 
could get millions readied.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.680
If some could fire a missile, 
others could use bombs.”

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.640
This is ultimately an age-old debate about who owns the Nile.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.240
In 1929 Egypt signed a treaty with Britain, a colonial power in Africa 

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:59.440
at the time, that basically guarantees its access to the Nile and gave

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:03.000
Egypt a veto over construction 
projects on the river.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.280
But Ethiopia didn’t sign that treaty.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.920
Then in 1999 all the Nile basin countries, including Egypt and Ethiopia

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.120
signed an agreement on
 managing water.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.400
But that deal was compromised in 2010  

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.240
when six of those countries decided 
to draw up another treaty. 

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.720
This time Egypt rejected it because it threatened its historic rights to water.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.520
It's Egypt's concern that if it moves into 

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:29.360
It's Egypt's concern that if it moves into 

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.080
then it will be left short
 of water resources

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:37.960
and also its control over the situation will be massively reduced.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:41.560
And as far as Sudan goes it’s sort 
of caught in the middle.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.720
Egypt is Sudan’s political ally 

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:46.080
but the government supports 
the Renaissance dam

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.880
because it’ll be a source of
affordable energy

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:50.560
and it will help regulate flooding.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:00.720
The good news is that Egypt, 
Ethiopia and Sudan

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.480
are in talks with one another.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:06.720
The bad news is that they still haven’t answered the tough questions. 

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.680
Questions like, when there’s a drought

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.160
will Ethiopia consider Egypt’s needs?

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.840
And when is a drought a drought?

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.320
Those conversations will 
only get tougher 

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.000
because more dams are being planned 

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.080
populations are growing and
 the climate is changing.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:24.080
All of which will make the Nile 
and the supply of water

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.040
increasingly worth fighting over.

